[' Here's a basic implementation of the requested module using `async/await` for handling asynchronous tasks and `cheerio` library for parsing and traversing the DOM:

```javascript
const cheerio = require('cheerio');
const axios = require('axios');

class DomProcessor {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
  }

  async fetchDom() {
    try {
      const response = await axios.get(this.url);
      return cheerio.load(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Error fetching DOM: ${error}`);
    }
  }

  filterElements(callback) {
    let $ = this._$ || this.fetchDom();
    this._$ = $; // Cache the Cheerio object for lazy loading

    const results = [];

    $('*').each((i, el) => {
      if (callback($(el))) {
        results.push(el);
      }
    });

    return results;
  }

  async processElements(elements) {
    const promises = elements.map(async (el) => {
      try {
        const $el = $(el);
        // Process the element here, for example, you can call a callback function on it or execute some other operation.
        console.log(`Processing element: ${$el}`);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Error processing element: ${error}`);
      }
    });

    await Promise.all(promises);
  }
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const DomProcessor = require('./DomProcessor');

const processor = new DomProcessor('https://example.com');
processor.processElements(processor.filterElements((element) => element.tagName === 'DIV')).then(() => console.log("Processing completed"));
```

This code defines a `DomProcessor` class with methods to fetch the DOM from an API endpoint, filter elements based on a user-provided callback function, and process the selected elements. The module supports lazy loading by caching the Cheerio object for future use when needed. Additionally, it handles errors gracefully by logging issues without halting the entire process.

This implementation provides a foundation for an extendable and maintainable solution, but further optimizations may be necessary depending on specific requirements such as memory management and performance considerations. For instance, you could limit the number of elements fetched at once or implement a depth-first search strategy to minimize memory usage when dealing with large or complex DOM trees.']

