["Here is a JavaScript module that dynamically loads and executes multiple external scripts within a web application:

```javascript
class ScriptLoader {
  constructor() {
    this.queue = [];
    this.loadedScripts = new Set();
    this.scriptElement = document.createElement('script');
    this.scriptElement.onload = this.handleOnLoad.bind(this);
  }

  addScript(src, callback) {
    if (!this.loadedScripts.has(src)) {
      const script = { src, callback };
      this.queue.push(script);
    }
  }

  load() {
    if (this.queue.length > 0) {
      const { src, callback } = this.queue.shift();

      // Load secure scripts only over HTTPS
      if (src.startsWith('https://')) {
        this.scriptElement.src = src;
      } else {
        throw new Error(`Script must be loaded over HTTPS: ${src}`);
      }

      document.head.appendChild(this.scriptElement);
    }
  }

  handleOnLoad() {
    const { src, callback } = this.queue.shift();
    this.loadedScripts.add(src);
    this.scriptElement.onload = null;

    try {
      // Run the callback, if any, in a try-catch block to handle potential errors
      callback();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Error executing script at ${src}:`, error);
    }
  }
}

// Usage example:
const scriptLoader = new ScriptLoader();

scriptLoader.addScript('https://cdn.example.com/script1.js', () => {
  // Your custom code here
});

scriptLoader.addScript('https://cdn.example2.com/script2.js', () => {
  // Your custom code here
});

// Start loading the scripts
setInterval(scriptLoader.load.bind(scriptLoader), 100);
```

This ScriptLoader class allows you to add multiple external scripts and their callback functions, ensuring that they are loaded over HTTPS for secure communication. The module handles cross-origin requests by checking if the script is loaded over HTTPS, and it manages CORS policies by throwing an error when attempting to load non-HTTPS resources.

The load() method checks if there are scripts in the queue, loads the next one, and appends the script element to the document head. The handleOnLoad method runs the callback once the script has finished loading, with any errors caught and logged. The setInterval ensures that scripts continue to be loaded even when the application is busy or experiencing network latency.

The module does not sanitize script content, but you can easily add a custom function for this purpose within the callback argument validation. You may also want to consider adding logging and monitoring capabilities by extending the console.error method or using a third-party library like Winston.js. Lastly, optimize the load() method's interval to balance between efficient execution and responsive application performance based on your specific use case and requirements."]

