[" Here's an implementation in C with a dynamic array of custom structures for network event logs, using a lock-free data structure (ring buffer) for efficient handling of varying loads and concurrent access. The external data store is represented by a simple file I/O function `write_log_to_file()` and `read_log_from_file()`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    time_t timestamp;
    int event_id;
    char message[256]; // Adjust the size based on expected message length
} log_entry;

#define RING_BUFFER_SIZE 10000 // Adjust as needed for expected load
volatile log_entry ring_buffer[RING_BUFFER_SIZE];
volatile size_t head = 0, tail = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void write_log_to_file(const char *filename, const log_entry *entry) {
    // Implement file I/O here
}

log_entry* get_log_entry_by_index(size_t index) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    size_t current_head = head;
    if (current_head >= RING_BUFFER_SIZE)
        current_head -= RING_BUFFER_SIZE;

    log_entry *entry = &ring_buffer[current_head + index];
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return entry;
}

void clear_logs() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    head = tail = 0;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void add_log(const log_entry *entry) {
    while ((head + 1) % RING_BUFFER_SIZE == tail)
        // This indicates that the buffer is full. Wait for some space to become available before adding a new entry.
        pthread_mutex_wait(&lock);

    ring_buffer[head] = *entry;
    head = (head + 1) % RING_BUFFER_SIZE;
}
```

The code above uses a lock-free data structure (ring buffer) for efficient handling of varying loads and concurrent access. However, it's essential to understand that using lock-free data structures can lead to higher memory usage due to the need for additional bookkeeping and potential cache line contention. In some scenarios, they may also introduce complexities in debugging and reasoning about the system behavior.

In contrast, traditional locking mechanisms (like mutexes) offer better debuggability, predictable behavior under high concurrency, but come with the overhead of acquiring and releasing locks that can impact performance for read-heavy operations.

Trade-offs between memory usage and speed need to be made based on the specific requirements and constraints of your application. To ensure the system can scale to support increased load over time, consider using techniques like power-of-two ring buffer sizes, cache line alignment, and tail chopping for efficient appending."]

